# A minimum Node is declared in `engines` across the root and every workspace.
# Without this it is advisory: npm prints a warning and installs anyway, so a
# contributor on an older Node gets a working install and discovers the
# mismatch later, as a runtime failure with no obvious cause. Verified both
# ways — on a supported Node nothing in the tree declares a narrower range, and
# below it `npm ci` stops with EBADENGINE naming the requirement.
#
# The second effect, learned afterwards: this also makes a *transitive*
# dependency's range binding. `jsdom@30.0.1` narrowed to
# `^22.22.2 || ^24.15.0 || >=26.0.0` with no change of ours, and from that moment
# every Node 24.0–24.14 failed the install outright — and so did every Node 25,
# which that range skips. That is the intended behaviour — a hard stop beats a
# tree that half works — but it means `engines.node` has to carry the real
# *range*, patch digits and gaps included, not a major and not a minimum.
# See `tests/architecture/the-node-floor-is-one-number.test.ts`.
engine-strict=true
